rastelli greicius gorno tempini laporte dedecker suain authelet heraud pierrot octave rescues amyloidogenic lesne kuskowski ashe clegg ashburner ridgway nuisance nuisance rockenstein veinbergs sagara mallory donohue jogi chertkow klunk koroshetz manly mayeux pasquini gottler grimmer ortner neitzel muhlau arfanakis nisbet ittner gotz sheehy berckel bnm pijnenburg gorno tempini iaccarino schonhaut janabi gorno tempini 2019a schonhaut lockhart ayakta janabi lazaris cantwell ohlsson strandberg palmqvist 2019b e601 e612 pascoal mathotaarachchi mohaddes benedet chamoun therriault propagates nasrallah pontecorvo devous navitsky galante salloway doraiswamy southekal pontecorvo devous navitsky salloway schaerf mcgeehan querfurth quiroz baena arboleda velasquez cosio lapoint rabinovici desanti kozauer rabinovici alkalay janabi bonasera mormino breteler mmb strooper frisoni salloway flier lockhart schonhaut janabi schwimmer strandberg palmqvist biofinder jogi ohlsson sepulcre sabuncu isla chhatwal seidler bitan sintini machulda spychalla drubach butters deteresa katzman orantes timmers verfaillie scj golla ssv barkhof boellaard flier loenhoud wink verfaillie scj twisk barkhof berckel flier vergara houben suain vanden dries rabinovici sheehy ayakta bentil vlassenko vaishnavi couture raichle mintun vogels leuzy cicognola smolek hromadka zilka benzinger friedrichsen aldea mcconathy mandelkow machulda spychalla drubach winblad amouyel andrieu brodaty cedazo minguez edvardsson defeating makaretz mcginnis gomperts sepulcre isla vasdev brainnet connectomics e68910 supplementarymaterials_bhaa184','Abstract A deeper understanding of the spatial relationships of β-amyloid (Aβ), tau, and neurodegeneration in Alzheimer’s disease (AD) could provide insight into pathogenesis and clinical trial design. We included 81 amyloid-positive patients (age 64.4 ± 9.5) diagnosed with AD dementia or mild cognitive impairment due to AD and available 11 C-PiB (PIB), 18 F-Flortaucipir (FTP), 18 F-FDG-PET, and 3T-MRI, and 31 amyloid-positive, cognitively normal participants (age 77.3 ± 6.5, no FDG-PET). W-score voxel-wise deviation maps were created and binarized for each imaging-modality ( W > 1.64, P < 0.05) adjusting for age, sex, and total intracranial volume (sMRI-only) using amyloid-negative cognitively normal adults. For symptomatic patients, FDG-PET and atrophy W-maps were combined into neurodegeneration maps (ND). Aβ-pathology showed the greatest proportion of cortical gray matter suprathreshold voxels (spatial extent) for both symptomatic and asymptomatic participants (median 94–55%, respectively), followed by tau (79–11%) and neurodegeneration (41–3%). Amyloid > tau > neurodegeneration was the most frequent hierarchy for both groups (79–77%, respectively), followed by tau > amyloid > neurodegeneration (13–10%) and amyloid > neurodegeneration > tau (6–13%). For symptomatic participants, most abnormal voxels were PIB+/FTP+/ND− (median 35%), and the great majority of ND+ voxels (91%) colocalized with molecular pathology. Amyloid spatially exceeded tau and neurodegeneration, with individual heterogeneities. Molecular pathology and neurodegeneration showed a progressive overlap along AD course, indicating shared vulnerabilities or synergistic toxic mechanisms. Keywords: amyloid, neurodegeneration, spatial extent, tau, topography Introduction Alzheimer’s disease (AD) is characterized by the pathologic accumulation of β-amyloid (Aβ) plaques and neurofibrillary tau tangles (NFT), which triggers downstream neurodegeneration and ultimately cognitive deterioration ( Querfurth and LaFerla 2010 ; Scheltens et al. 2016 ; Winblad et al. 2016 ). Converging data from autopsy studies show that molecular pathology (Aβ and tau) accumulation follows different spatiotemporal trajectories along the AD course ( Braak and Braak 1991 ; Thal et al. 2002 ; Braak et al. 2006 ; Brettschneider et al. 2015 ). Aβ plaques are first observed in isocortical association areas, spreading progressively to allocortex, limbic regions, brainstem, and cerebellum. NFT pathology is first found in locus coeruleus and entorhinal and transentorhinal cortex, spreading progressively to hippocampal regions, lateral temporal cortex, association cortex, and primary unimodal cortex in late stages ( Braak and Braak 1991 ; Braak et al. 2006 ). Positron emission tomography (PET) studies have largely replicated these observations in vivo, showing extensive neocortical Aβ accumulation early in the preclinical stage, with age-related tau accumulation initially localized to the medial temporal lobes, subsequently spreading stereotypically into temporal, parietal, and frontal cortex in amyloid-positive individuals ( Johnson et al. 2016 ; Schöll et al. 2016 ; Pontecorvo et al. 2017 ; Ossenkoppele, Smith, et al. 2019b ). There is a spatial and temporal decoupling also between neurodegeneration and Aβ plaques, suggesting that the influence of Aβ on neuronal integrity may be indirect or remote ( Rabinovici et al. 2010 ; La Joie et al. 2012 ; Lehmann et al. 2013 ; Altmann et al. 2015 ; Pascoal et al. 2019 ). Tau-PET studies have demonstrated a tighter relationship between NFT accumulation and neurodegeneration, with regional tau PET uptake correlating with atrophy, glucose hypometabolism, and clinical deficits across the spectrum of disease severity ( Bischof et al. 2016 ; Brier et al. 2016 ; Cho et al. 2016 ; Dronse et al. 2016 ; Johnson et al. 2016 ; Ossenkoppele et al. 2016 ; Schöll et al. 2016 ; Sepulcre et al. 2016 ; Wang et al. 2016 ; Bejanin et al. 2017 ; LaPoint et al. 2017 ; Pontecorvo et al. 2017 ; Schöll et al. 2017 ; Xia et al. 2017 ; Iaccarino et al. 2018 ; Jack et al. 2019 ; Ossenkoppele, Smith, et al. 2019b ; Pontecorvo et al. 2019 ; Timmers et al. 2019 ; La Joie et al. 2020 ). These and other findings have increasingly supported the hypothesis that Aβ acts as a facilitator of NFT spreading ( Hurtado et al. 2010 ; Gomes et al. 2019 ; Shin et al. 2019 ; Vergara et al. 2019 ), which in turn may mediate the neurotoxic effects of Aβ on brain structure and cognition ( Ittner et al. 2010 ; Leroy et al. 2012 ). A better understanding of the these relationships could provide greater insight into disease pathogenesis and inform the integration of multimodal neuroimaging into therapeutic trials and drug development. While previous studies focused on regional intensities of these processes, we investigated absolute and relative spatial extent, that is, the proportion of involved cortical gray matter and topography of Aβ-pathology, tau-pathology, and neurodegeneration by using in vivo PET and structural MRI imaging. This approach offers a complementary mechanistic angle to the study of molecular pathology to neurodegeneration relationships and may offer novel insights into AD regional vulnerabilities and pathogenesis. Here, we report the in vivo spatial relationships between molecular pathology and neurodegeneration in a group of subjects along the AD continuum. We hypothesized that 1) the spatial extent of increased Aβ-PET would exceed tau-PET elevation, which in turn would exceed the extent of neurodegeneration, consistent with the predicted temporal ordering of biomarker progression ( Jack et al. 2018 ); 2) the spatial extent of tau-PET abnormalities would decrease with older age and vary according to AD clinical subtype ( Dronse et al. 2016 ; Ossenkoppele et al. 2016 ; Cho et al. 2017 ; Schöll et al. 2017 ; Xia et al. 2017 ; Phillips et al. 2018 ; Sintini et al. 2019 ; Whitwell et al. 2019 ); and 3) the spatial overlap between Aβ, tau, and neurodegeneration would increase with disease progression and correlate with cognitive status. Materials and Methods Participants Symptomatic AD We retrospectively included research participants recruited from the Alzheimer’s Disease Research Center at the University of California San Francisco, Memory and Aging Center, who 1) met clinical criteria for either mild cognitive impairment (MCI) due to AD ( Albert et al. 2011 ) or probable AD dementia ( McKhann et al. 2011 ) as determined by a multidisciplinary research evaluation including medical and neurological history, physical examination, structured caregiver interview, and neuropsychological testing; 2) were confirmed to be amyloid-positive by 11 C-PiB amyloid-PET (PIB-PET) at semiquantification (neocortical 11 C-PIB SUVR composite score >1.21) ( Villeneuve et al. 2015 ); 3) underwent PIB-PET, 18 F-Flortaucipir tau-PET (FTP-PET), 18 F-FDG-PET measuring glucose metabolism (FDG-PET) and 3T structural MRI (sMRI) within 12 months; 4) did not meet criteria for other neurodegenerative conditions and did not have a significant history of traumatic brain injury, systemic diseases, significant cerebrovascular disease or recent substance abuse. We included 81 symptomatic AD patients (age mean ± standard deviation (SD) 64.4 ± 9.5, N = 41 females) meeting these criteria. Furthermore, N = 17 and N = 16 patients met additional criteria for logopenic variant of primary progressive aphasia (lvPPA) and posterior cortical atrophy (PCA), respectively ( Gorno-Tempini et al. 2011 ; Crutch et al. 2017 ). Preclinical AD A group of N = 31 amyloid-positive cognitively normal (preclinical AD) participants of the Berkeley Aging Cohort Study ( Harrison et al. 2019 ), recruited through advertisement in the community (age mean ± SD 77.3 ± 6.5, N = 16 females), were also included. These participants had available PIB-PET, FTP-PET, and 3T MRI (all within 12 months, except 1 subject who had the MRI at about 1.5 years from PIB-PET and FTP-PET) and were included in order to study the earliest stage of AD pathogenesis and thus capture the full pathophysiological continuum. Cognitively Normal Controls We included two different groups of PIB-PET negative, cognitively normal control (Amy− CN) participants from the Berkeley Aging Cohort Study for statistical comparisons. Inclusion criteria for the CN were 1) no cognitive complaints or objective cognitive deficit beyond normal for age; 2) negative PIB-PET at semiquantification (see above). The first CN group (Amy− CN Group 1) included N = 46 amyloid-negative participants with available PIB-PET, FTP-PET, and 3T sMRI (age mean ± SD 69.35 ± 18.7, N = 16 females) and was considered for the PIB-PET, FTP-PET and 3T sMRI-based comparisons. The second CN group (Amy− CN Group 2) included N = 38 amyloid-negative participants with FDG-PET and structural MRI (either 1.5 or 3T) (age mean ± SD 69.2 ± 16.2, N = 17 females) and was considered only for the FDG-PET comparisons. The CN groups shared N = 13 subjects and, when excluding them, the two groups did not differ significantly on any demographic measure. Written informed consent was obtained from all patients or their surrogates. The study was approved by the University of California (San Francisco and Berkeley) and Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory (LBNL) institutional review boards for human research. MRI Acquisition Structural MRI scans were acquired either at the UCSF Neuroimaging Center (symptomatic AD group) or at the Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory/Henry H. Wheeler Jr Brain Imaging Center at UC Berkeley (cognitively normal participants, both preclinical AD and control groups). Symptomatic patients underwent a high-resolution T1-weighted magnetization-prepared rapid gradient echo (MPRAGE) sequence acquired on either a 3T Siemens Tim Trio scanner ( N = 30/81, sagittal slice orientation; 160 slices; slice thickness = 1.0 mm; in-plane resolution = 1.0 × 1.0 mm; matrix = 240 × 256; repetition time = 2300 ms; echo time = 2.98 ms; flip angle = 9°) or on a 3T Siemens Prisma Fit ( N = 51/81, sagittal slice orientation; 160 slices; slice thickness = 1.0 mm; in-plane resolution = 1.0 × 1.0 mm; matrix = 240 × 256; repetition time = 2300 ms; echo time = 2.9 ms; inversion time = 900 ms; flip angle = 9°) at UCSF. Cognitively normal participants underwent either a high-resolution T1-weighted MPRAGE sequence acquired on a 1.5T Siemens Magnetom Avanto (Amy− CN Group 2; sagittal slice orientation; 160 slices; slice thickness = 1.0 mm; slice thickness = 1.0 mm; in-plane resolution = 1.0 × 1.0 mm; matrix = 256 × 240; repetition time = 2110 ms; echo time = 3.58 ms; flip angle = 15°) or a high-resolution T1-weighted MPRAGE sequence acquired on a 3T Siemens TIM Trio scanner (preclinical AD, Amy− CN Group 1; sagittal slice orientation; 160 slices; slice thickness = 1.0 mm; slice thickness = 1.0 mm; in-plane resolution = 1.0 × 1.0 mm; matrix = 256 × 240; repetition time = 2300 ms; echo time = 2.98 ms; flip angle = 9°) at UC Berkeley. MRI Processing MRIs were segmented using Statistical Parametric Mapping version 12 (SPM12, https://www.fil.ion.ucl.ac.uk/spm/ ) software. Total intracranial volume (TIV) was estimated using SPM12 ( Malone et al. 2015 ). All segmented MRIs from symptomatic and preclinical AD patients as well as Amy− CN Group 1 ( N = 158 3T MRIs in total) were used to create a study-specific template via Diffeomorphic Anatomical Registration using Exponentiated Lie algebra (DARTEL) processing with SPM12 ( Ashburner 2007 ). Segmented gray matter maps were then warped to the Montreal Neurological Institute (MNI) space preserving concentration (modulated) for subsequent volumetric analyses. Finally, the modulated, warped gray matter maps were smoothed using a 8 mm 3 isotropic, full width at half maximum Gaussian kernel. MRIs from the Amy− CN Group 2 ( N = 38) were warped to the previously created DARTEL template and used to obtain transformation parameters to MNI space to warp the respective FDG-PET scans (see below). These images were not incorporated in the DARTEL template because 1) they included a mix of 1.5T and 3T scans and 2) they were not used for volumetric comparisons but only to process the FDG-PET data (see below). All the images were visually checked for macroscopical distortions or warping failures. No subjects were excluded. PET Acquisition Participants (symptomatic, preclinical AD, and CN groups) underwent PET scans with 18 F-FTP, 11 C-PIB, and 18 F-FDG at Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory on a Siemens Biograph 6 Truepoint PET/CT scanner in 3D acquisition mode. Attenuation correction was performed using a low-dose CT/transmission scan acquired prior to all the PET scans. A dynamic PiB-PET scan was acquired for 90 min immediately after intravenous injection of ~15 mCi. FTP-PET scan acquisition followed intravenous injection of ~10 mCi of 18 F-FTP and varied in their timing, although always including the 80–100 min post injection window, used for statistical analyses (see below). FDG-PET was acquired for 30 min (6 × 5 min frames) after 30 min of eyes-open quiet rest following intravenous injection of ~10 mCi 18 F-FDG. Scans were reconstructed using an ordered subset expectation maximum algorithm with weighted attenuation and were smoothed using a 4 mm Gaussian kernel with scatter correction (calculated image resolution 6.5 × 6.5 × 7.25 mm using Hoffman phantom). PET Processing Reconstructed PET frames were realigned for motion correction and coregistered with reslicing to participants’ T 1 -weighted MRIs. Voxel-wise standardized uptake value ratio (SUVR) images were created in native MRI space for FTP-PET (80–100 min, inferior cerebellar gray matter reference region), PIB-PET (50–70 min, whole cerebellar gray), and FDG-PET (30–60 min, pons). Reference regions were defined via FreeSurfer-derived (v5.3) Desikan-Killiany atlas regions as well as SUIT template for cerebellum ( Diedrichsen 2006 ). Neocortical composite PIB-PET SUVRs were calculated as weighted mean of a large neocortical area including temporal, parietal, and frontal regions ( Villeneuve et al. 2015 ). PET images were warped to MNI space with the MRI-, DARTEL-based transformations. Finally, PET images were smoothed using a 4.8 mm isotropic, full width at half maximum Gaussian kernel. These differential smoothing filters for MRI and PET data were applied to obtain about the same final resolution (i.e . , about 8 mm 3 isotropic) for the different modalities. To avoid any processing steps that could artificially boost the sensitivity to detect differences between symptomatic/preclinical AD patients and controls for FTP-PET and PIB-PET relative to FDG-PET, we did not correct PET data for partial volume effects. W-score Maps Estimation and Processing Explicit Mask Definition All analyses were run on the whole cortical gray matter (GM). We first created a whole cortical GM region of interest (ROI) including frontal, parietal, occipital, temporal, and limbic lobes as defined in the Neuromorphometrics Atlas provided in SPM12 (Neuromorphometrics Inc, http://www.neuromorphometrics.com , provided under academic subscription), which was then smoothed to obtain a final ~8 mm 3 resolution and masked with the smoothed mean of the warped segmented gray matter maps, obtaining the final mask. W-maps Estimation and Thresholding Voxel-wise W-maps were estimated using previously described methods ( La Joie et al. 2012 ; Ossenkoppele et al. 2015 ; van Loenhoud et al. 2017 ). This approach allows to generate single-subject maps (W-maps) for each modality showing statistical deviations (W-scores) from normal controls considering specific covariates. W-scores distribution is analogous to Z-scores distribution and can thus be evaluated considering properties of the Gaussian curve ( La Joie et al. 2012 ; Ossenkoppele et al. 2015 ; van Loenhoud et al. 2017 ). We considered age and sex as covariates for all modalities and added TIV for atrophy W-maps. FDG-PET hypometabolism and MRI-atrophy W-maps were sign-inverted in order to make the interpretation of W-scores consistent across modalities, that is, higher W-scores equal more severe biomarker alteration. Next, W-maps for each subject and modality were binarized at P < 0.05 (i.e., W > 1.64). We selected this more permissive statistical threshold to avoid overcorrection for the neurodegeneration biomarkers, which were less sensitive than molecular markers. For symptomatic AD patients, binary FDG-PET and MRI atrophy maps were merged in a “neurodegeneration” (ND) map, deeming as positive voxels that were hypometabolic or atrophic or both. For preclinical AD participants, only the MRI atrophy W-map was considered as neurodegeneration measure since FDG-PET was not available. W-sum Maps Creation For each subject, PiB-PET, FTP-PET, and neurodegeneration binary W-maps were summed in a single image (W-sum map) (see Supplementary Fig. 1 for details). The creation of the W-sum maps allowed to define the classification of each cortical GM voxel according to its PIB±/FTP±/ND± status, thus testing spatial overlap between imaging modalities, at the single-subject level. Statistical Analysis Custom MATLAB (v. R2015a) scripts were used to extract the number of suprathreshold voxels for both 1) single PIB, FTP, ND/MRI modalities and 2) each PIB±/FTP±/ND± combined voxel classification. Spatial Extent was estimated as the proportion of suprathreshold voxels out of the entire cortical GM mask, as follows: Hierarchies of relative spatial extents were assigned to each subject by sorting biomarkers according to their individual spatial extents. For instance, if the extent of amyloid-PET abnormalities was larger than tau-PET, which in turn was more extended than neurodegeneration, the subject would be assigned a PIB > FTP > ND (i.e . , amyloid > tau > neurodegeneration) hierarchy. Statistical comparisons and plotting were performed with R software ( www.R-project.org , v. 3.5.1 for Mac OsX) and additional packages, including ggplot2 , ppcor , psych , eulerr, and rcompanion . We employed Fisher’s exact test for group-comparisons of discrete variables, Mann–Whitney U-tests for independent two-group comparisons, Kruskal–Wallis test for independent 3+ group comparisons, paired Wilcoxon Rank sum test for dependent two-group comparisons, Friedman Rank sum test for dependent 3+ group comparisons, and Spearman rho for bivariate correlations. P -values were corrected for multiple comparisons with an Holm–Bonferroni correction. Results Symptomatic AD patients were younger than the preclinical AD and CN participants ( P < 0.001), had higher neocomposite PIB-PET SUVRs ( P < 0.001) and lower scores on the Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE, P < 0.001), and were more frequently carriers of the Apolipoprotein E (APOE) ε4 allele compared with the CN groups (Fisher’s exact test, P < 0.001). Preclinical AD participants were older than the Amy− CN Group2 ( P < 0.001) and had a higher proportion of APOE ε4 carriers compared with both CN groups ( P < 0.001). See Table 1 for details. Table 1. Demographics Symptomatic AD Preclinical AD CN — — Group 1 Group 2 N 81 31 46 38 Age 62 (58–72) a 79 (74–80) e 76 (69–81) 74 (72–77) Sex (F, %) 41 (50.6%) 15 (48.4%) 30 (65.2%) 21 (55.3%) Education (years) 16 (16–18) 16 (16–18) 16 (16–18) 16 (16–18) APOE e4 Carrier ( N carriers, %) 34 (57%) c 17 (58.6%) d 6 (15%) 7 (18.4%) Composite PiB-PET SUVR 2.1 (2–2.3) b 1.5 (1.3–2) d 1.1 (1.1–1.2) 1.1 (1.1–1.2) PiB-PET centiloids 109.1 (92.8–126.6) b 50 (25.3–92.4) d 12.6 (7.8–15.3) 12.9 (8.8–15.8) Amyloid (PIB-PET) positive (yes, %) 81 (100%) 31 (100%) 0 (0%) 0 (0%) Clinical variant lvPPA 17/81 (21%) — — — PCA 16/81 (20%) — — — MMSE 23 (19–26) a 29 (28–30) 29 (28–30) 29 (28–30) CDR (<1/≥1) 42/39 (52%/48%) — — — CDR sum of boxes 4 (3–5) — — — Interval FTP-PIB (absolute no. days) 0 (0–0) 0 (0–50) 0(0–0) — Interval FTP-MRI 21 (2–75) 28 (7–76.5) 33.5 (14–75.5) — Interval PIB-MRI 24 (2–75) 47 (11.5–91.5) 34 (17.2–65.5) — Interval FTP-FDG 8 (1–40) — — — Interval FDG-MRI 42 (4–113) — — 19.5 (7.2–47.2) Open in a new tab Table showing summary of demographics, clinical, and acquisition time interval data for symptomatic AD, preclinical AD, and both control groups. Continuous variables are shown as median (interquartile range). Percentages for discrete variables were calculated over total of available data. APOE data were missing for 21 symptomatic AD patients, 2 preclinical AD participants, and 6 cognitively normal subjects from CN Group 1. Legend: AD, Alzheimer’s disease; CN, cognitively normal; APOE, apolipoprotein E; PIB, 11 C-PIB-PET; lvPPA, logopenic variant of primary progressive aphasia; PCA, posterior cortical atrophy; MMSE, Mini-Mental State Examination; CDR, clinical dementia rating; FTP, 18 F-flortaucipir-PET; MRI, magnetic resonance imaging a Symptomatic AD < Preclinical AD, Amy− CN Group 1, Amy− CN Group 2: P < 0.001 b Symptomatic AD > Preclinical AD, Amy− CN Group 1, Amy− CN Group 2: P < 0.001 c Symptomatic AD > Amy− CN Group 1, Amy− CN Group 2: P < 0.001 d Preclinical AD > Amy− CN Group 1, Amy− CN Group 2: P < 0.001 e Preclinical AD > Amy− CN Group 2: P < 0.001 Group-level Hierarchies and Topography of Amyloid-PET, Tau-PET, and Neurodegeneration Changes Across the AD Continuum Consistent with our hypothesis, at the group-level we found that the spatial extent was greatest for PIB-PET (group median 94% of GM involved, InterQuartile Range—IQR 90–96%), followed by FTP-PET (median 79%, IQR 64–90%) and neurodegeneration (median 41%, IQR 25–51%) ( P < 0.001, all pairwise significant at P < 0.001) ( Fig. 1 A ). The same result was observed within individual AD clinical subgroups (see Supplementary Fig. 2 and Supplementary Table 1 ). Within ND measures, the spatial extent of FDG-PET changes exceeded the extent of sMRI changes ( P < 0.001, see Supplementary Fig. 3 ). Preclinical AD participants showed similar findings, with the spatial extent of PIB-PET abnormalities being the greatest (median 55%, IQR 32–89%), followed by FTP-PET (median 11%, IQR 6–27%) and sMRI atrophy (median 3%, IQR 1–4%) ( P < 0.001, all pairwise significant at P < 0.001) ( Fig. 1 B ). Thus, at the group-level, the most frequent observed hierarchy of spatial extents was PIB > FTP > ND/MRI, that is, amyloid > tau > neurodegeneration, for all participants in the AD continuum. Similarly, the amyloid-negative cognitively normal participants (Amy− CN Group 1) showed greatest spatial extent for amyloid ( P = 0.002, no pairwise contrast surviving multiple comparisons correction), though without a clearly preferred hierarchy of spatial extents ( Supplementary Fig. 4 ). Figure 1. Open in a new tab Spatial extent of Aβ-, tau-pathology, and neurodegeneration. Boxplots showing spatial extents for each modality for both symptomatic patients ( A ) and preclinical AD subjects ( B ). Single points for each participant are added to show the underlying distribution. ***All the pairwise differences were significant at P < 0.001 (see text for details). Legend: GM, gray matter; AD, Alzheimer’s disease; FTP, 18 F-flortaucipir-PET; PIB, 11 C-PIB-PET; ND, neurodegeneration; MRI, magnetic resonance imaging. We also tested the impact of manipulating different statistical thresholds, that is, different critical W-scores, and confirmed that these did not affect the relative spatial extent among modalities for both symptomatic and preclinical AD groups ( Supplementary Fig. 5 ). Additionally, we investigated the relationships between individual biomarker spatial extents and age at PET scan for both symptomatic and preclinical AD participants. In symptomatic patients, FTP-PET was significantly correlated with age (Spearman rho = −0.67, corrected P < 0.001), with a significant but weaker association found with PIB-PET (rho = −0.29, corrected P = 0.04). Neurodegeneration spatial extent was not significantly associated with age (rho = −0.24, corrected P -value = 0.11). In preclinical AD participants, none of the spatial extents were significantly associated with age (FTP, rho = 0.07, corrected P -value = 1; PIB, rho = −0.13, corrected P -value = 1; MRI, rho = 0.24, corrected P -value = 1) ( Supplementary Fig. 6 ). Finally, we tested a possible MRI scanner effect on the spatial extent estimation of both sMRI atrophy and ND and found no significant effect of scanner (sMRI atrophy: t -value = 1.1, P = 0.27; ND: t -value = 0.23, P = 0.82) ( Supplementary Fig. 7 ). To evaluate variability in individual spatial patterns of Aβ, tau and neurodegeneration, we computed frequency maps showing the degree of overlap, within group, of the individual biomarker abnormalities ( Fig. 2 ). For essentially all symptomatic patients, Aβ accumulation was present in the majority of the neocortical GM, relatively sparing primary sensorimotor cortex, visual cortex, and medial temporal regions. In the preclinical AD group, PIB-PET alterations converged in dorsolateral prefrontal and medial frontal regions, as well as in medial/lateral parietal regions ( Fig. 2 ). FTP-PET elevations were consistently observed in temporoparietal and frontal regions for almost all the symptomatic patients, but were less homogeneous in preclinical AD, mostly converging in lateral, inferior temporal, and medial parietal regions ( Fig. 2 ). Neurodegeneration patterns in symptomatic patients converged in lateral parietal regions, followed by lateral temporal, medial parietal and dorsolateral prefrontal areas. Atrophy patterns in preclinical AD participants were markedly heterogeneous, with essentially no brain regions showing gray matter reductions in more than 25% of the group ( Fig. 2 ). As a control analysis, frequency maps were generated for typical and atypical AD variants in the symptomatic group (see Supplementary Fig. 8 ). These maps confirmed the higher regional convergence of tau pathology and neurodegeneration as compared with essentially invariant topography of Aβ accumulation. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Voxel-wise frequency maps of biomarker alteration. Figure showing voxel-wise frequency maps for each biomarker alteration for both symptomatic (upper panel) and preclinical AD participants (lower panel). Values indicate the percentage of subjects showing abnormality in each voxel. Color scale was limited to 25% as minimum for the sake of visualization. Frequency maps are overlaid on an ICBM152 smoothed template with BrainNet Viewer ( Xia et al. 2013 ). Legend: AD, Alzheimer’s disease; FTP, 18 F-flortaucipir-PET; PIB, 11 C-PIB-PET; ND, neurodegeneration; MRI, magnetic resonance imaging. Individual Subject Hierarchies of PIB-PET, FTP-PET, and Neurodegeneration Changes Across the AD Continuum Multiple hierarchies of spatial extents were observed at the individual level, for both preclinical and symptomatic participants (see Table 2 and Fig. 3 for a summary). In symptomatic patients, the most frequent hierarchy was PIB > FTP > ND (79%), followed by FTP > PIB > ND (13%) and PIB > ND > FTP (6%) ( Fig. 3 A ). ND > PIB > FTP and FTP > ND > PIB were both found in a single patient (1%). Patients belonging to the different hierarchies differed significantly in age and MMSE at FTP-PET time, with PIB > ND > FTP patients being older than PIB > FTP > ND and FTP > PIB > ND patients (corrected P = 0.01 and P = 0.05, respectively) and with a higher MMSE score compared with FTP > PIB > ND patients ( P = 0.05) ( Supplementary Fig. 9 ). In preclinical AD, the most common hierarchy was PIB > FTP > MRI (77%) followed by PIB > MRI > FTP (13%) and FTP > PIB > MRI (10%) ( Fig. 3 B ). In preclinical AD the different hierarchies were not significantly associated with demographics or baseline cognitive status, including the handful of preclinical individuals with a high spatial extent of FTP. Table 2. Relative hierarchies of biomarkers spatial extent Symptomatic AD Preclinical AD Hierarchy N (%) Hierarchy N (%) PIB > FTP > ND 64 (79%) PIB > FTP > MRI 24 (77%) FTP > PIB > ND 10 (13%) PIB > MRI > FTP 4 (13%) PIB > ND > FTP 5 (6%) FTP > PIB > MRI 3 (10%) FTP > ND > PIB 1 (1%) ND > PIB > FTP 1 (1%) Open in a new tab Table showing frequencies of observed relative hierarchies of individual biomarker spatial extents for symptomatic and preclinical AD subgroups. Legend: AD, Alzheimer’s disease; FTP, 18 F-flortaucipir-PET; PIB, 11 C-PIB-PET; ND, neurodegeneration; MRI, magnetic resonance imaging Figure 3. Open in a new tab Hierarchies of spatial extents. Spaghetti plots showing rankings of spatial extents for both symptomatic ( A ) and preclinical AD participants ( B ). Individual relative hierarchies are shown in gray in the background. Colored lines connect median group values relative to the assigned hierarchies (see text). Legend: GM, gray matter; AD, Alzheimer’s disease; FTP, 18 F-flortaucipir-PET; PIB, 11 C-PIB-PET; ND, neurodegeneration; MRI, magnetic resonance imaging. Voxel-wise Extent of Molecular Pathology and Neurodegeneration Across the AD Continuum In symptomatic AD patients, we found that a median of 98% of cortical GM voxels showed at least one biomarker alteration, whereas 72% of cortical GM voxels showed at least one biomarker alteration in preclinical AD ( Supplementary Table 2 ). The most frequent voxel classification in symptomatic AD was PIB+/FTP+/ND−, which accounted for 35% of the cortical GM. This was closely followed by PIB+/FTP+/ND+ (34%) and PIB+/FTP−/ND− (12%). All other voxel classifications individually accounted for less than 2% of cortical GM ( Fig. 4 A and Supplementary Table 2 ). In preclinical AD, the most frequent voxel classification was PIB+/FTP−/MRI− (41%), followed by PIB+/FTP+/MRI− (9%) and PIB−/FTP+/MRI− (2%) ( Fig. 4 A and Supplementary Table 2 ). In symptomatic AD patients, 91% of the neurodegeneration-positive voxels colocalized with Aβ and tau pathology, whereas in preclinical AD 47% of atrophic voxels colocalized with Aβ pathology, with only 7% colocalizing with both Aβ and tau pathology ( Fig. 4 B ). The proportion of voxels that showed neurodegeneration despite lack of colocalized Aβ or tau was about 2% for the symptomatic AD patients and 45% for the preclinical AD participants ( Fig. 4 B ). Figure 4. Open in a new tab Voxel classifications combining Aβ, tau, and neurodegeneration alterations. Figure showing the median proportion for each PIB±/FTP±/ND± voxel classification at the whole cortical GM level ( A ) and the molecular pathology status of neurodegeneration-positive voxels ( B ) for both symptomatic and preclinical AD participants. ( A ) Venn diagrams show proportions of voxel classifications over the whole cortical GM for both symptomatic (upper diagram) and preclinical AD participants (lower diagram). The size of the diagrams has been scaled according to the proportion of voxels spared by both molecular pathology and neurodegeneration (median 2% for symptomatic AD, 28% for preclinical AD, see text and Supplementary Table 2 for details). The ND data for the preclinical AD participants refer to atrophy changes only. ( B ) Barplots show the molecular pathology status for all the neurodegeneration-positive voxels in the whole cortical GM. Proportions shown are calculated over the total amount of neurodegeneration-positive voxels (therefore totaling to 100% for both groups). Legend: GM, gray matter; AD, Alzheimer’s disease; FTP, 18 F-flortaucipir-PET; PIB, 11 C-PIB-PET; ND, neurodegeneration. Figure 5 shows topographical frequency maps for the three most frequent voxel classifications. In symptomatic patients, PIB+/FTP+/ND− tended to cluster in lateral/medial fronto-insular and medial occipital regions, whereas PIB+/FTP−/ND− voxels were mostly found in primary sensorimotor, medial frontal, and anterior cingulate regions. Of note, PIB+/FTP+/ND+ voxels were the most homogenous, converging in temporoparietal and frontal regions, closely resembling the well-known AD-signature neurodegeneration pattern. In preclinical AD participants, PIB+/FTP−/MRI− voxels were observed in the whole cortical GM, although peaking in dorsolateral prefrontal, medial prefrontal, and medial parietal regions. PIB+/FTP+/MRI− voxels converged in inferior lateral temporal and lateral and medial parietal regions, although with marked heterogeneity, with no voxels colocalizing in more than 25% of the participants (see Fig. 5 and Supplementary Table 2 ). Figure 5. Open in a new tab Voxel-wise frequency maps of voxel classifications. Figure showing voxel-wise frequency maps for voxel classifications for both symptomatic patients (upper panel) and preclinical AD participants (lower panel). Only the three most frequent voxel classifications for each group are shown (see text and Supplementary Table 2 ). Values indicate the percentage of participants showing the specific PIB±/FTP±/ND(MRI)± classification in each voxel. Color scale was limited to 25% as minimum for the sake of visualization. Frequency maps are overlaid on an ICBM152 smoothed template with BrainNet Viewer ( Xia et al. 2013 ). Legend: AD, Alzheimer’s disease; FTP, 18 F-flortaucipir-PET; PIB, 11 C-PIB-PET; ND, neurodegeneration; MRI, magnetic resonance imaging. Relationships between Spatial Extent of Single or Combined Biomarker Changes and Cognitive Status In symptomatic patients, when considering individual modalities, the proportion of ND+ voxels showed the strongest association with MMSE (ρ = −0.65, corrected P < 0.001), followed by the proportion of FTP+ voxels (ρ = −0.57, corrected P < 0.001) ( Fig. 6 ). The proportion of PIB+ voxels was not significantly associated with MMSE (ρ = −0.06, corrected P = 0.77). When combining modalities, the proportion of PIB+/FTP+/ND+ voxels showed the highest correlation with MMSE (ρ = −0.69, corrected P < 0.001). No significant associations between MMSE and single or combined biomarker abnormalities extents were observed in the preclinical AD group. See Supplementary Table 3 for details. Figure 6. Open in a new tab Relationships between spatial extents and global cognition. Scatterplots showing relationships in symptomatic participants between MMSE total score and spatial extent of neurodegeneration ( A ) or spatial extent of voxels positive to both molecular pathologies and neurodegeneration ( B ). Red lines show linear fit and respective confidence intervals (gray shaded area). Legend: GM, gray matter; AD, Alzheimer’s disease; FTP, 18 F-flortaucipir-PET; PIB, 11 C-PIB-PET; ND, neurodegeneration; MMSE, Mini-Mental State Examination. Discussion We applied structural MRI and PET markers of Aβ, tau, and glucose metabolism to assess the spatial extent and overlap of molecular pathology and neurodegeneration across the AD continuum ( Jack et al. 2018 ). We found that, on average, Aβ accumulation is more widespread than neurofibrillary tangle pathology throughout the evolution of the disease. The topography and extent of tau pathology varied along disease progression, with early focal accumulation in preclinical stages followed by extended neocortical spread in symptomatic stages. Neurodegeneration was more tightly related to tau than Aβ and was strongly associated with cognition in the symptomatic stage. We found spatially distinct patterns of Aβ and tau in preclinical AD, with a progressive topographical overlap in later disease stages, ultimately synergizing with local neurodegeneration resulting in cognitive decline. Several potential mechanisms have been proposed to contribute to regional brain vulnerability to Aβ accumulation. Earliest Aβ deposition appears in medial/lateral parietal and frontal areas, which are characterized by higher Amyloid Precursor Protein (APP) gene expression ( Grothe et al. 2018 ), by higher aerobic glycolysis compared with other neocortical regions ( Vlassenko et al. 2010 ) and by being part of specific functional networks, that is, default-mode and frontoparietal networks ( Buckner et al. 2005 ; Vlassenko et al. 2010 ; Myers et al. 2014 ; Grothe et al. 2016 ). Based on our data on preclinical and symptomatic AD, early amyloid detection tends to be more regionally specific in earlier clinical stages but rapidly involves large portions of cortical gray matter, consistent with neuropathological staging ( Thal et al. 2002 ). As for tau pathology, postmortem studies in AD have revealed a specific vulnerability of excitatory neurons, both in the entorhinal cortex (large pyramidal neurons in layer II) ( Hyman et al. 1984 ) and in the neocortex ( Fu et al. 2019 ), possibly related to their high metabolic demand, dense dendritic structure, length of unmyelinated axons, and gene expression profiles ( Fu et al. 2018 ; Vogels et al. 2019 ). Tau accumulation patterns highly resemble brain functional networks, supporting the transneuronal spreading hypothesis ( Jones et al. 2017 ; Cope et al. 2018 ; Franzmeier et al. 2019 , 2020 ; Ossenkoppele, Iaccarino, et al. 2019a ). Our findings show that tau initially accumulates focally, with further cortical spreading as the disease progresses. The spread of tau is more regionally specific than the spread of Aβ, with late accumulation being primarily confined to AD-signature temporoparieto-frontal regions. The convergence of medial temporal tau with diffuse neocortical Aβ seems to enable the spread of tau, initially into inferior temporal cortex and from there into additional neocortical regions, possibly via transsynaptic spread ( Wang and Mandelkow 2016 ; Quiroz et al. 2018 ; Adams et al. 2019 ; Jack et al. 2019 ). Notwithstanding their known and observed progressive topographical overlap, in our patients, some brain regions showed Aβ but not tau accumulation, even in later disease stages. These findings suggest that Aβ pathology is not necessarily followed locally by tangle pathology in AD pathogenesis, and that local seeds of misfolded tau, spreading from temporal regions, may be necessary for local cooccurrence. When Aβ and tau spatially converge, however, they are likely to enter an interaction loop, as shown in animal studies ( Ittner and Götz 2011 ; Bloom 2014 ; Nisbet et al. 2015 ), with Aβ promoting tau accumulation ( Hurtado et al. 2010 ; Gomes et al. 2019 ; Shin et al. 2019 ; Vergara et al. 2019 ), which in turn mediates Aβ deposition and neurotoxicity ( Ittner et al. 2010 ; Leroy et al. 2012 ). Diffuse Aβ accumulation followed by progressively overlapping tau accumulation was found at the single-subject level in the majority of symptomatic and preclinical AD patients. Few participants deviated from this canonical hierarchy, instead showing tau deposition spatially exceeding Aβ accumulation. While the difference in spatial extents in these cases was small (median of 6%), this pattern was systematically observed in patients with younger age at tau-PET scan (age mean ± SD 59 ± 11) as compared with patients with the canonical hierarchy (age mean ± SD 64 ± 9). These findings suggest that younger patients tend to have a more extensive tau burden as compared with older patients, which is consistent with the known negative association between tau-PET binding and age at onset/PET, especially in frontoparietal regions ( Ossenkoppele et al. 2016 ; Cho et al. 2017 ; Schöll et al. 2017 ; Whitwell et al. 2019 ). When considering neurodegeneration markers, we observed very frequent topographical concordance with both molecular pathologies, supporting a local pathology interaction toward neurotoxicity, in keeping with previous data ( Bischof et al. 2016 ; Brier et al. 2016 ; Cho et al. 2016 ; Dronse et al. 2016 ; Johnson et al. 2016 ; Ossenkoppele et al. 2016 ; Schöll et al. 2016 , 2017 ; Sepulcre et al. 2016 ; Wang et al. 2016 ; Bejanin et al. 2017 ; LaPoint et al. 2017 ; Pontecorvo et al. 2017 ; Xia et al. 2017 ; Iaccarino et al. 2018 ; Ossenkoppele, Smith, et al. 2019b ; Timmers et al. 2019 ; La Joie et al. 2020 ). Notwithstanding their topographical concordance, neurodegeneration extent was markedly more restricted on average compared with Aβ and tau, consistent with synaptic dysfunction and neuronal loss being downstream events in AD pathogenesis. It is also possible that molecular pathology and neurodegeneration had different variability in the CN groups, with the latter having a wider range and thus being harder to detect with our approach. Whether both Aβ and tau are mechanistically required to trigger local neurodegeneration is not yet fully understood. Amyloid pathology can promote local neurotoxicity, especially in its soluble oligomeric form ( Lesné et al. 2013 ). Similarly, cellular studies have demonstrated that neurons bearing neurofibrillary tangles show signs of synaptic loss ( Callahan et al. 1999 ; Ginsberg et al. 2000 ), with soluble oligomeric tau possibly being the primary toxic species ( Congdon and Sigurdsson 2018 ). Additionally, tau accumulation has been shown in humans to locally predict future atrophy, to a much larger degree than Aβ accumulation ( La Joie et al. 2020 ). Our findings are consistent with these claims, showing that the spatial extent of tau accumulation typically exceeds and contains the pattern of neurodegeneration. However, a small number of symptomatic AD patients and preclinical AD participants showed a neurodegeneration extent exceeding tau accumulation, in the context of diffuse Aβ pathology. These patients were among the oldest, and it is thus possible that they had higher degrees of comorbidities contributing to neurodegeneration ( Rabinovici et al. 2016 ; Boyle et al. 2019 ; James and Bennett 2019 ), such as TDP-43 ( Nelson et al. 2019 ), alpha-synuclein or mild cerebrovascular disease. Therefore, especially in older individuals, Aβ plaques may independently coexist or rather interplay with other non-tau pathologies in triggering neurodegeneration. Supporting this possibility, studies have shown that Aβ pathology can induce α-synuclein oligomerization ( Masliah et al. 2001 ), TDP-43 phosphorylation and accumulation ( Herman et al. 2011 ) and endothelial dysfunction ( Koizumi et al. 2016 ), all in turn associated with neuronal death ( Fricker et al. 2018 ). The proportion of molecular pathology and neurodegeneration-positive voxels in the gray matter showed the strongest association with global cognitive status in symptomatic patients. This is in keeping with the known independent and combined effects of Aβ or tau pathology and neurodegeneration on cognition in AD ( Terry et al. 1991 ; Bejanin et al. 2017 ; Digma et al. 2019 ; Krance et al. 2019 ; Mattsson et al. 2019 ; Ossenkoppele, Smith, et al. 2019b ; Pascoal et al. 2019 ). Despite normal cognition, a few individuals with preclinical AD showed a high spatial extent of molecular pathology. These subjects had limited atrophy, reinforcing that the cooccurrence of neurodegeneration is a key driver of cognitive dysfunction. These data overall support the notion that combining pathological, structural and functional markers of synaptic dysfunction and neurodegeneration offers the highest accuracy in monitoring disease progression ( Jack et al. 2018 ; Jagust 2018 ). An analogous approach could be applied in clinical trials to evaluate whether the treatment slowed (or even halted) the progressive spatial spread of molecular pathology and neurodegeneration. This would offer an alternative measure compared with the conventional approaches, for example, testing a decrease in magnitude of biomarker abnormalities in given regions, also strongly associating with global cognitive status. Strengths and Limitations This study has several strengths. We applied a multimodal neuroimaging approach to investigate spatial biomarker relationships, offering an alternative informative window onto AD pathogenetic processes. We included patients covering the entire AD continuum, from preclinical to symptomatic AD phases, and took advantage of their clinical heterogeneity by adopting a single-subject analytical approach to complement group-level analyses. We developed a voxel-wise approach to define the spatial overlap of pathology and neurodegeneration, identifying regions with shared vulnerabilities across patients and providing novel evidence on possible synergistic Aβ/tau spatial interactions associated with local neurodegeneration and ultimately cognitive decline. This study also has limitations. First, it is cross-sectional and by definition we were not able to track the spatial dynamics of neuroimaging changes over time. However, we included participants spanning the full clinical spectrum of AD continuum, thus modeling different stages of the AD course. Additionally, our results are based on a specific statistical threshold to define biomarkers as abnormal. While the overall extent of biomarker abnormalities was by definition threshold-dependent, we found that the relative relationships between biomarkers were robust at varying thresholds, strengthening our findings. Symptomatic AD patients were scanned with different 3T MRI scanners, though there was no significant effect of scanner on spatial extent. Another caveat is that our sample was skewed to younger age-of-onset and atypical AD variants. While not representative of AD in the general population, a younger-onset cohort is less likely to harbor copathologies such as limbic-predominant age-related TDP-43 encephalopathy neuropathological changes (LATE-nc), synuclein or cerebrovascular disease, thus offering a cleaner framework to investigate Aβ/tau/neurodegeneration relationships. Still, we could not specifically evaluate the presence of concomitant proteinopathies in our cohort, such as TDP-43 or synuclein pathology, and therefore we cannot exclude that the amount and localization of neurodegeneration was influenced by their presence, especially in older subjects. In conclusion, regional vulnerabilities drive spatially divergent early Aβ and tau accumulation in preclinical AD stages. The topographical convergence and interactions of molecular pathology in later disease stages are associated with colocalized synaptic dysfunction and neuronal loss, eventually driving cognitive decline. Individual variations in the spatial relationships between molecular pathology and neurodegeneration exist, indicating that AD pathogenesis dynamically interacts with individual brain architectures. Neurodegeneration remains the strongest and most accessible biomarker related to global cognition, highlighting its relevance as a disease monitoring tool, with some added value when combined with molecular pathology. Supplementary Material SupplementaryMaterials_bhaa184 Click here for additional data file. (1.7MB, docx) Contributor Information Leonardo Iaccarino, Memory and Aging Center, Department of Neurology, Weill Institute for Neurosciences, University of California San Francisco, San Francisco, CA 94158, USA. Renaud La Joie, Memory and Aging Center, Department of Neurology, Weill Institute for Neurosciences, University of California San Francisco, San Francisco, CA 94158, USA. Lauren Edwards, Memory and Aging Center, Department of Neurology, Weill Institute for Neurosciences, University of California San Francisco, San Francisco, CA 94158, USA. Amelia Strom, Memory and Aging Center, Department of Neurology, Weill Institute for Neurosciences, University of California San Francisco, San Francisco, CA 94158, USA. Daniel R Schonhaut, Memory and Aging Center, Department of Neurology, Weill Institute for Neurosciences, University of California San Francisco, San Francisco, CA 94158, USA; Helen Wills Neuroscience Institute, University of California Berkeley, Berkeley, CA 94720, USA. Rik Ossenkoppele, Memory and Aging Center, Department of Neurology, Weill Institute for Neurosciences, University of California San Francisco, San Francisco, CA 94158, USA; Department of Neurology and Alzheimer Center, Neuroscience Campus Amsterdam, VU University Medical Center, Amsterdam 1081 HV, The Netherlands. Julie Pham, Memory and Aging Center, Department of Neurology, Weill Institute for Neurosciences, University of California San Francisco, San Francisco, CA 94158, USA. Taylor Mellinger, Memory and Aging Center, Department of Neurology, Weill Institute for Neurosciences, University of California San Francisco, San Francisco, CA 94158, USA. Mustafa Janabi, Molecular Biophysics and Integrated Bioimaging, Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory, Berkeley, CA 94720, USA. Suzanne L Baker, Molecular Biophysics and Integrated