r A, Chakraborty BS. Interim analysis: a rational approach of decision making in clinical trial. J. Adv. Pharm. Technol. Res. 2016; 7: 118–122. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Ciolino JD, Kaizer AM, Bonner LB. Guidance on interim analysis methods in clinical trials. J. Clin. Transl. Sci. 2023; 7: e124. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Wason J, Brocklehurst P, Yap C. When to keep it simple – adaptive designs are not always useful. BMC Med. 2019; 17: 1–7. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. Kapur J, Elm J, Chamberlain JM et al . Randomized trial of three anticonvulsant medications for status epilepticus. N. Engl. J. Med. 2019; 381: 2103–2113. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 27. Parmar MK, Sydes MR, Cafferty FH et al . Testing many treatments within a single protocol over 10 years at MRC Clinical Trials Unit at UCL: multi‐arm, multi‐stage platform, umbrella and basket protocols. Clin. Trials 2017; 14: 451–461. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 28. Park JJH, Harari O, Dron L, Lester RT, Thorlund K, Mills EJ. An overview of platform trials with a checklist for clinical readers. J. Clin. Epidemiol. 2020; 125: 1–8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 29. Woodcock J, LaVange LM. Master protocols to study multiple therapies, multiple diseases, or both. N. Engl. J. Med. 2017; 377: 62–70. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 30. The Adaptive Platform Trials Coalition . Adaptive platform trials: definition, design, conduct and reporting considerations. Nat. Rev. Drug Discov. 2019; 18: 797–807. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 31. Saville BR, Berry SM. Efficiencies of platform clinical trials: a vision of the future. Clin. Trials 2016; 13: 358–366. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 32. Koenig F, Spiertz C, Millar D et al . Current state‐of‐the‐art and gaps in platform trials: 10 things you should know, insights from EU‐PEARL. eClinicalMedicine 2024; 67: 102384. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 33. Lee KM, Wason J. Including non‐concurrent control patients in the analysis of platform trials: is it worth it? BMC Med. Res. Methodol. 2020; 20: 165. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 34. Marschner IC, Jones M, Totterdell JA et al . Transparent reporting of adaptive clinical trials using concurrently randomised cohorts. BMJ Medicine 2023; 2: e000497. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 35. Ouma LO, Grayling MJ, Wason JMS, Zheng H. Bayesian modelling strategies for borrowing of information in randomised basket trials. J. R. Stat. Soc. Ser. C. Appl. Stat. 2022; 71: 2014–2037. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 36. Angus DC, Berry S, Lewis RJ et al . The REMAP‐CAP (randomized embedded multifactorial adaptive platform for community‐acquired pneumonia) study. Rationale and design. Ann. Am. Thorac. Soc. 2020; 17: 879–891. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 37. REMAP‐CAP: A Randomised, Embedded, Multi‐factorial, Adaptive Platform Trial for Community‐Acquired Pneumonia. Available from URL: https://www.remapcap.org/ 38. Klasnja P, Hekler EB, Shiffman S et al . Microrandomized trials: an experimental design for developing just‐in‐time adaptive interventions. Health Psychol. 2015; 34S: 1220–1228. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 39. Lillie EO, Patay B, Diamant J, Issell B, Topol EJ, Schork NJ. The n‐of‐1 clinical trial: the ultimate strategy for individualizing medicine? Per. Med. 2011; 8: 161–173. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Data Availability Statement Data sharing not applicable to this article as no datasets were generated or analysed during the current study.',151,'2025-10-15 11:23:04','202